# Session Summary — March 7, 2026

## What we did

Iterated through six versions of the chat selector mockup (v1–v6), significantly enriching the conversation list from a basic navigation table into a knowledge management surface. Also explored the idea of a command-palette-style chat switcher modal as an alternative/complement to the full-screen selector.

## Key Design Decisions

### Chat Selector Enrichment

**AI Summary inline under every chat title.** Each row shows the title on the first line and a truncated AI-generated summary on the second line by default. Summaries are auto-generated after the first user→assistant exchange (same call that generates the title, or a parallel lightweight call). Users don't need to expand anything to see what a conversation is about.

**Global view toggle: Summary | Notes | Highlights | Branches.** A segmented control in the page header switches what the second line of every row displays. Summary is the default. When switched to Notes, all rows show their notes inline. Same for Highlights and Branches. This replaces the per-row expand/collapse pattern from earlier iterations, giving the table a consistent visual rhythm.

**Per-row override via interactive count badges.** Each row shows small pill badges (like the existing branch badge) for notes count and highlights count next to the title. Clicking a badge overrides that specific row's view independently of the global toggle. Clicking it again snaps the row back to the global view. This gives power users quick per-row access without disrupting the overall view.

**Notes system.** Notes are created either inside the conversation UI (anchored to a specific message) or from the chat list table. Message-anchored notes show `(Message 14)` in light grey text. Notes created from the table have no label — the absence signals it's a general note. Notes are editable and deletable from both the conversation and the table. The "+ Add a note" button is always visible in notes view but rendered at 45% opacity (de-emphasized), transitioning to full opacity on hover for a smooth, non-jittery interaction.

**Highlights system.** Highlights are created inside the conversation UI by selecting text. They appear in the chat list as read-only items with a colored left bar indicating the highlight color. Multi-line highlights have a taller, fully opaque bar; single-line highlights have a shorter, partially transparent bar. Each highlight has a copy button on hover.

**Tags/Topics column.** Replaced the Size column. Tags are auto-generated from conversation content (same pattern as auto-title). Displayed as stacked colored pills, vertically centered. Non-sortable for V1 — sorting is a V2 consideration if users request it.

**De-emphasized empty rows.** When the global view is Notes, Highlights, or Branches, rows with no content for that view drop to 45% opacity. Rows with content stay at full strength, drawing the eye to what matters. No "No notes yet" or "No highlights yet" text — just the title and the de-emphasis.

### Flat Branch Model

**Branches are peers, not children.** All conversations live at the same level in the table regardless of whether they're a parent or branch. This was a deliberate departure from the nested/indented model in earlier mockups (`chat-selector-v2.jsx`). Rationale: branches frequently outgrow their parents and become the primary conversation, making visual subordination misleading.

**Branch relationships shown via the Branches global view.** When the Branches toggle is active, branch conversations show "↳ branched from [Parent Title]" with a colored left bar (family color). Parent conversations show a list of their child branches. Standalone conversations with no branch relationships are de-emphasized.

**Family colors.** Related conversations (parent + all branches) share a color from a predefined palette. This color is used in the Branches view as the left bar indicator, providing visual grouping without hierarchy.

**No "from" subtitles in default view.** Branch origin information only appears in the Branches view. In Summary, Notes, and Highlights views, every conversation looks identical — clean title and content. No persistent visual hierarchy.

**Branch count badge on parent rows.** Parents show an interactive GitBranch badge with the count of children. Clicking it overrides that row to show its branches (same pattern as notes/highlights badges).

### Bulk Actions

**Smart pin toggle.** A single Pin icon in the bulk action bar. The system detects if any selected chats are unpinned → shows "Pin" tooltip; if all are already pinned → shows "Unpin". Replaces the separate Pin/Unpin icons.

**Regenerate summaries.** Added to the bulk action bar next to Pin and Delete. Triggers AI summary regeneration for all selected conversations.

### Other UI Changes

**Lighter metadata text.** Size, Edited, Created column values and headers use `#C0C8D4` instead of `#6B7C93`, pushing metadata further into the background.

**Simplified footer.** Shows only conversation count and total storage size. Removed highlights/notes/branches counts since the global toggle now surfaces that information.

**Click target zones (design intent, not yet in mockup).** Title text click → opens the conversation. Badge clicks → toggle inline views. Add note button → opens editor. Empty row space → opens conversation (or does nothing — TBD during implementation).

### Command Palette Chat Switcher (Explored, Not Implemented)

Discussed a modal-within-conversation approach to chat selection (keyboard shortcut like ⌘K). Would allow quick switching without leaving the current chat. Decided to sleep on it and potentially implement as a complement to the full selector, not a replacement. The full selector has too much functionality (notes, highlights, branches, bulk actions) to replace with a modal.

